  }
 ],
 "questions": [
  {
   "n": 1,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "CAJOLE means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "force someone with threats"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "scold harshly"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "persuade by flattery"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "ignore completely"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "Cajole = persuade by flattery. The word's Latin root is caveola, a small cage — you trap someone sweetly rather than by force. Why the others tempt: “force someone with threats” and “scold harshly” are its opposite (coerce, berate); “ignore completely” drops the influence entirely.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 2,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "IMMINENT means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "about to happen"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "very old"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "hard to believe"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "impossible to find"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "Imminent = about to happen. Note the -minent root (as in “imminent danger”): it hangs over you now. Why the others tempt: “very old” confuses it with eminent or ancient, “hard to believe” with incredible, “impossible to find” with inscrutable.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "SIEGE means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "a celebration"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "a peace treaty"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "a sudden retreat"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "a blockade of a place by an army"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "explanation": "Siege = a blockade of a place by an army, from sedere, “to sit” — the army sits outside until the town gives in. Why the others tempt: the -iege spelling resembles “siege of celebration” parties, and a siege is the opposite of both a peace treaty and a retreat.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "CURTAIL means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "to lengthen"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "to cut short"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "to hide"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "to praise"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "Curtail = to cut short (curt- “short”). Why the others tempt: “to lengthen” is the direct antonym (extend); “to hide” borrows the disguise sense of cover; “to praise” is the meaning of extol, a different verb.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "CANDID means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "frank and honest"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "secretive"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "shy"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "angry"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "Candid = frank and honest. In Latin candidus meant “white, bright”, hence a plain, open answer. Why the others tempt: “secretive” is the exact opposite, and it is easy to blend with “candid camera” trickiness (deceptive) rather than openness.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 6,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "FRUGAL means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "wasteful"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "generous"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "careful with money"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "very rich"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "Frugal = careful with money, thrifty, from frux, “fruit/produce” — getting full value. Why the others tempt: “wasteful” is the antonym (extravagant); “generous” confuses thrift with meanness; “very rich” is about how much you have, not how you spend.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 7,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "DICHOTOMY means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "a union of similar things"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "a long journey"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "a polite request"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "a sharp division into two opposite parts"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "explanation": "Dichotomy = a sharp division into two opposites (di- “two” + tomia “cutting”). Why the others tempt: “a union of similar things” is the antonym; the “-tomy” ending is mistaken for “-nomy” (economy) or for a path/travel sense.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 8,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "VERBOSE means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "silent"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "using more words than needed"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "speaking very softly"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "speaking briefly"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "Verbose = using more words than needed — verbose comes from verbum plus the -ose suffix meaning “full of”. Why the others tempt: “silent” and “speaking briefly” are opposites (terse, laconic); “speaking very softly” confuses wordiness with volume.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 9,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "LENIENT means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "not strict; merciful"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "cruel"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "very angry"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "careless"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "Lenient = not strict, merciful (from lenire, “to soften”). Why the others tempt: “cruel” is the opposite (severe, harsh); “careless” confuses a deliberate leniency with negligence.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 10,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "METICULOUS means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "lazy"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "hurried"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "very careful about small details"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "forgetful"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "Meticulous = very careful about small details. Why the others tempt: “hurried” and “lazy” both miss the painstaking sense; “forgetful” confuses care with memory, and meticulous attention is precisely the opposite of forgetting details.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 4
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 11,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "“خود مختار” means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "reciprocal"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "autonomous"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "obsolete"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "notorious"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "خود مختار = self-governing, i.e. autonomous (khud = self, mukhtar = in charge). Why the others tempt: reciprocal (with ہم آہنگ / باہمی) is the vocabulary word for mutual; obsolete (فرسودہ) and notorious (بدنام) are nearby list words but unrelated to self-rule.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 12,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "HUMILIATION means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "عزت افزائی"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "خوشی"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "حیرت"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "ذلت و رسوائی"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "explanation": "Humiliation = ذلت و رسوائی (disgrace). Why the others tempt: عزت افزائی means the opposite — increasing someone's honour; خوشی (joy) and حیرت (astonishment) pick up the emotion of the moment without the loss of dignity.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 13,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "“مبہم” means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "ambiguous"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "candid"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "abundant"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "diligent"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "مبہم = unclear, with two possible meanings → ambiguous. Why the others tempt: candid (صاف گو) is about being plain-spoken, the opposite of ambiguous; abundant (کثیر) and diligent (محنتی) are list words with no link to vagueness.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 14,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "BENEVOLENT means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "بدنیت"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "کنجوس"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "مہربان و نیک دل"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "جلد باز"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "Benevolent = well-wishing: bene- “good” + volens “wishing” → مہربان و نیک دل. Why the others tempt: بدنیت (ill-intentioned) and کنجوس (miserly) are opposites; جلد باز (hasty) replaces the “well-wishing” root with a false “bold” reading.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 15,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "“بے سود” means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "vivid"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "futile"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "eloquent"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "zealous"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "بے سود = without profit/use → futile. Why the others tempt: vivid (روشن) and eloquent (فصیح) both sound positive, the reverse of futility; zealous (سرگرم) is enthusiasm, not uselessness.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 16,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "NOTORIOUS means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "نیک نام"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "گمنام"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "بہادر"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "بدنام"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "explanation": "Notorious = famous for something bad → بدنام. Why the others tempt: نیک نام (of good repute) is the opposite; گمنام (unknown) confuses fame with obscurity; بہادر (brave) is a virtue, not a bad reputation.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 17,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "“ناگزیر” means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "trivial"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "lenient"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "inevitable"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "prudent"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "ناگزیر = unavoidable → inevitable (from guzar, “to pass/escape”; what cannot be escaped). Why the others tempt: trivial (معمولی) and lenient (نرم) are unrelated list words; prudent (محتاط) sounds careful but means cautious, not certain to happen.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 18,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "HOSTILITY means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "دشمنی"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "دوستی"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "رحم"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "صبر"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "Hostility = enmity → دشمنی (hostis = enemy). Why the others tempt: دوستی (friendship) is the antonym; رحم (mercy) and صبر (patience) are calm virtues, not active ill will.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 19,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "“فصیح” means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "verbose"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "obscure"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "meticulous"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "eloquent"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "explanation": "فصیح = fluent and persuasive → eloquent. Why the others tempt: verbose (لفظوں کا زیادہ استعمال) is wordiness without force; obscure (مبہم) is the opposite of clear speech; meticulous (محقق) is about detail, not delivery.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 20,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "PRUDENT means:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "لاپرواہ"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "دانشمند و محتاط"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "غصہ ور"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "سست"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "Prudent = wise and careful about the future → دانشمند و محتاط. Why the others tempt: لاپرواہ (careless) and غصہ ور (irritable) are opposites of prudence; سست (lazy) confuses caution with slowness.",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 21,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "GENIALITY:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "cordiality"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "hostility"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "rapidity"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "beauty"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "Geniality = friendly warmth → cordiality. Why the others tempt: hostility is its antonym; rapidity and beauty are distractor words chosen only for their -ity ending.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 22,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "HEINOUS:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "trivial"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "pleasant"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "atrocious"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "ordinary"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "Heinous = shockingly wicked → atrocious. Why the others tempt: trivial and ordinary both mean “not serious”, the direct opposite of a heinous crime; pleasant is the opposite of wicked.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 6
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 23,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "PRAGMATIC:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "dreamy"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "practical"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "careless"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "emotional"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "Pragmatic = practical, judging by results rather than theory. Why the others tempt: dreamy and emotional are the idealistic opposite; careless confuses “practical” with “sloppy”, but a pragmatic person is usually careful about what works.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 6
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 24,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "SANCTITY:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "cruelty"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "wealth"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "speed"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "holiness"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "explanation": "Sanctity = holiness (sanctus = holy). Why the others tempt: cruelty is a moral opposite; wealth and speed are unrelated nouns with a similar rhythm.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 6
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 25,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "CONFRONT:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "avoid"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "face"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "hide"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "forget"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "explanation": "Confront = face boldly (com- “together” + frons “forehead” — forehead to forehead). Why the others tempt: avoid and hide are the antonyms; forget removes the issue instead of meeting it.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 6
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 26,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "OBSTRUCTION:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "hindrance"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "support"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "gift"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "signal"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A"
   ],
   "explanation": "Obstruction = blockage → hindrance. Why the others tempt: support is the opposite (it clears the way); gift and signal are unrelated fillers.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 6
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 27,
   "type": "single",
   "question": "ABUNDANT:",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "scarce"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "tiny"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "plentiful"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "hidden"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "explanation": "Abundant = plentiful, more than enough. Why the others tempt: scarce is its antonym; tiny describes size, not quantity; hidden describes visibility.",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "sourceRef": {
    "page": 6
   }
  },
  {
